Frame buffer pool — 1514-byte slots for L2 frame I/O
====================================================

inet was originally built with `var frame: [1514]byte` and
`var reply: [1500]byte` scattered through every handler. That
cost ~1.5 KB of stack per call and hardcoded the MTU at every
site. The pool replaces that pattern with a small fixed array
of buffers and three helpers — `inet_frame_alloc` hands out an
index, `inet_frame_release` returns it, `inet_frame_buf`
resolves the index to a `*byte`. inet is single-threaded, so a
bitmap-on-int allocator is plenty; eight slots is several
deeper than any actual call chain.

The pool is its own sibling now that the sysl analyzer's
cross-file `var` resolution works (analyzer fix from the
oskit.posix split: `registerSiblingForwardDeclsFrom` now covers
`VarDeclAST`). Forward calls from `inet.lsysl` and other
siblings reach `inet_frame_alloc` / `_buf` / `_release` the
same way they reach any other in-module helper.

To grow the MTU later, flip the literal `1514` in the storage
decl and in callers that pass a recv length explicitly; sysl
array-size literals must be hardcoded, but the pool API's
`*byte` signatures keep handlers indifferent.

    module oskit.servers

    import oskit.ipc.{ipc_reply}


### Frame buffer pool (Phase 3 chunk 1)

inet was built with `var frame: [1514]byte` / `var reply: [1500]byte`
scattered through the handlers. That cost ~1.5 KB of stack per call
and hardcoded the MTU at every site; bumping to jumbo frames meant
editing every literal. The pool replaces that pattern with a small
fixed array of buffers and three helpers — `inet_frame_alloc` hands
out an index, `inet_frame_release` returns it, and `inet_frame_buf`
resolves the index to a `*byte`. inet is single-threaded, so a
bitmap-on-int allocator is plenty; eight slots is several deeper
than any actual call chain.

The pool lives in this file (not `inet.lsysl`) because cross-file
VAR access still confuses the sysl analyzer per
`feedback_sysl_cross_unit.md` — the helpers must sit with the var
they read. Forward calls from `inet.lsysl` (one direction) work fine.

To grow the MTU later, flip the literal `1514` in the storage decl
and in callers that pass a recv length explicitly; sysl array-size
literals must be hardcoded, but the pool API's `*byte` signatures
keep handlers indifferent.

    val INET_FRAME_POOL_N   = 8
    val INET_FRAME_BUF_SIZE = 1514

    var inet_frame_pool: [8][1514]byte
    var inet_frame_used: [8]int
    var inet_frame_high_water: int = 0
    var inet_frame_alloc_fails: int = 0

Allocate the lowest-indexed free slot. Returns the index, or -1 if
the pool is exhausted (which would be a bug — pool sizing is meant
to outrun any realistic call chain).

    inet_frame_alloc() -> int
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_FRAME_POOL_N
            if inet_frame_used[i] == 0
                inet_frame_used[i] = 1
                var live = 0
                var j = 0
                while j < INET_FRAME_POOL_N
                    if inet_frame_used[j] != 0
                        live += 1
                    j += 1
                if live > inet_frame_high_water
                    inet_frame_high_water = live
                return i
            i += 1
        inet_frame_alloc_fails += 1
        -1

Release a slot. Out-of-range indices are ignored so callers can pass
through a `-1` returned by a failed alloc without branching.

    inet_frame_release(idx: int)
        if idx >= 0 && idx < INET_FRAME_POOL_N
            inet_frame_used[idx] = 0

Resolve an index to its backing `*byte`. Out-of-range indices fall
through to the slot-0 buffer so a buggy caller doesn't fault on a
null deref; the alloc-fail path will already be visible in stats.

    inet_frame_buf(idx: int) -> *byte
        if idx < 0 || idx >= INET_FRAME_POOL_N
            return &inet_frame_pool[0][0]
        &inet_frame_pool[idx][0]

Frame-pool stats — exposed via `INET_CMD_FRAME_POOL_STATS=51`.
Lets a test verify no handler is leaking pool slots and that the
pool's high-water mark stays comfortably below capacity. `used`
counts slots whose `inet_frame_used[i] != 0`; `high` is the running
max of `used` across server lifetime; `cap` is `INET_FRAME_POOL_N`;
`fails` counts requests that returned -1.

Wire:

  request:  cmd(1) = 1 byte
  reply:    status(1) + used_be32(4) + high_be32(4)
            + cap_be32(4) + fails_be32(4) = 17 bytes

    inet_handle_frame_pool_stats(sender: int, _buf: *byte)
        var used = 0
        var i = 0
        while i < INET_FRAME_POOL_N
            if inet_frame_used[i] != 0
                used += 1
            i += 1
        val high = inet_frame_high_water
        val cap  = INET_FRAME_POOL_N
        val fails = inet_frame_alloc_fails
        var reply: [17]byte
        reply[0] = byte(0)
        reply[1] = byte((used >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[2] = byte((used >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[3] = byte((used >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[4] = byte( used        & 0xff)
        reply[5] = byte((high >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[6] = byte((high >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[7] = byte((high >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[8] = byte( high        & 0xff)
        reply[ 9] = byte((cap >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[10] = byte((cap >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[11] = byte((cap >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[12] = byte( cap        & 0xff)
        reply[13] = byte((fails >> 24) & 0xff)
        reply[14] = byte((fails >> 16) & 0xff)
        reply[15] = byte((fails >>  8) & 0xff)
        reply[16] = byte( fails        & 0xff)
        ipc_reply(sender, reply, 17)

